{"name":"Thunder & Lightning","hex":"#f9f5db","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"thunder","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The loud rumbling, cracking, or crashing sound caused by expansion of rapidly heated air around a lightning bolt.","A deep, rumbling noise resembling thunder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce thunder; to sound, rattle, or roar, as a discharge of atmospheric electricity.","To make a noise like thunder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thunder"},{"term":"lightning","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flash of light produced by short-duration, high-voltage discharge of electricity within a cloud, between clouds, or between a cloud and the earth.","A discharge of this kind."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extremely fast or sudden; moving (as if) at the speed of lightning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lightning"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thunder","blocks":[{"text":"Thunder is the sound caused by lightning. Depending upon the distance from and nature of the lightning, it can range from a long, low rumble to a sudden, loud crack. The sudden increase in temperature and hence pressure caused by the lightning produces rapid expansion of the air in the path of a lightning bolt. In turn, this expansion of air creates a sonic shock wave, often referred to as a \"thunderclap\" or \"peal of thunder\". The scientific study of thunder is known as brontology and the irrational fear (phobia) of thunder is called brontophobia."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder"},{"title":"Thunder and Lightning (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Thunder and Lightning is the twelfth and final studio album by Irish hard rock band Thin Lizzy, released on 4 March 1983. Guitarist John Sykes was hired to replace Snowy White after 1981's Renegade, and Sykes helped to provide a heavier sound and guitar tone than Thin Lizzy had used on previous albums. However, the bulk of the songwriting was completed before he joined the band. Keyboard player Darren Wharton also offered a stronger musical influence to Thin Lizzy's final studio album, co-writing many of the tracks including \"Some Day She Is Going to Hit Back\", and the final single \"The Sun Goes Down\". A farewell tour followed the album's release, followed by the live album Life."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/dd/Thin_Lizzy_-_Thunder_and_Lightning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder_and_Lightning_(album)"},{"title":"Ampersand","blocks":[{"text":"The ampersand, also known as the and sign, is the logogram &, representing the conjunction \"and\". It originated as a ligature of the word et."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Et_sign.svg/330px-Et_sign.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ampersand"},{"title":"Lightning","blocks":[{"text":"Lightning is a natural phenomenon consisting of electrostatic discharges occurring through the atmosphere between two electrically charged regions. One or both regions are within the atmosphere, with the second region sometimes occurring on the ground. Following the lightning, the regions become partially or wholly electrically neutralized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Port_and_lighthouse_overnight_storm_with_lightning_in_Port-la-Nouvelle.jpg/330px-Port_and_lighthouse_overnight_storm_with_lightning_in_Port-la-Nouvelle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightning"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Pay_and_Display_sign_with_Tironian_et_for_Irish_agus.jpg/500px-Pay_and_Display_sign_with_Tironian_et_for_Irish_agus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Pay_and_Display_sign_with_Tironian_et_for_Irish_agus.jpg/500px-Pay_and_Display_sign_with_Tironian_et_for_Irish_agus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bilingual Irish street sign, with ⁊ used to represent agus (first line) and & used to represent and (second line)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APay_and_Display_sign_with_Tironian_et_for_Irish_agus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ampersand_Handwriting_3.jpg/120px-Ampersand_Handwriting_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ampersand_Handwriting_3.jpg/250px-Ampersand_Handwriting_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ampersand_Handwriting_3.jpg/120px-Ampersand_Handwriting_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmpersand_Handwriting_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Albertus_481_100_1936_%285637417032%29.jpg/120px-Albertus_481_100_1936_%285637417032%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Albertus_481_100_1936_%285637417032%29.jpg/250px-Albertus_481_100_1936_%285637417032%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Albertus_481_100_1936_%285637417032%29.jpg/120px-Albertus_481_100_1936_%285637417032%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlbertus_481_100_1936_(5637417032).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo